jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fd8a8ee/content/data/versions/610.json", "paragraphs": ["英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな疾患であるため、保護者の3割弱しか症状を認識できると感じていない。年間約1900人が診断され、15～24歳でも毎年2300人が新たに診断されている。小児がんは成長に伴い成人よりはるかに急速に進行する。", "13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母ジェマさんは、疲労や軽い運動後の息切れを「運動不足や学校後の疲れ」と捉えていたという。しかし、あざが現れたことで異変を悟り、10日以内に診断が確定した。", "小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、持続し、悪化または拡大する症状の集積に注意するよう促す。極度の疲労、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きくなり続けるしこりなどだ。専門家は「3のルール」を提唱する。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["在英国，癌症是15岁以下儿童病死原因之首，但由于属于罕见病，不到三成的家长觉得自己能识别症状。每年约1900名儿童被诊断，15至24岁人群每年也有2300人新确诊。儿童癌症随成长比成人进展快得多。", "13岁的阿梅莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓系白血病。母亲杰玛说，她把疲劳和轻微运动后气喘当作“缺乏锻炼或放学后的疲惫”。但出现淤青后她察觉不对劲，10天内确诊。", "儿科肿瘤医生鲍勃·菲利普斯教授提醒注意持续、恶化或扩大的症状聚集：极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因淤青、不断变大的肿块等。专家提出“三原则”：若有三个持续症状、三次就诊、或三周无法解释的症状，就应考虑转诊专科。"], "body": "英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな疾患であるため、保護者の3割弱しか症状を認識できると感じていない。年間約1900人が診断され、15～24歳でも毎年2300人が新たに診断されている。小児がんは成長に伴い成人よりはるかに急速に進行する。\n\n13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母ジェマさんは、疲労や軽い運動後の息切れを「運動不足や学校後の疲れ」と捉えていたという。しかし、あざが現れたことで異変を悟り、10日以内に診断が確定した。\n\n小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、持続し、悪化または拡大する症状の集積に注意するよう促す。極度の疲労、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きくなり続けるしこりなどだ。専門家は「3のルール」を提唱する。", "body_zh": "在英国，癌症是15岁以下儿童病死原因之首，但由于属于罕见病，不到三成的家长觉得自己能识别症状。每年约1900名儿童被诊断，15至24岁人群每年也有2300人新确诊。儿童癌症随成长比成人进展快得多。\n\n13岁的阿梅莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出急性髓系白血病。母亲杰玛说，她把疲劳和轻微运动后气喘当作“缺乏锻炼或放学后的疲惫”。但出现淤青后她察觉不对劲，10天内确诊。\n\n儿科肿瘤医生鲍勃·菲利普斯教授提醒注意持续、恶化或扩大的症状聚集：极度疲劳、面色苍白、不明原因淤青、不断变大的肿块等。专家提出“三原则”：若有三个持续症状、三次就诊、或三周无法解释的症状，就应考虑转诊专科。", "text": "英国では15歳未満の子どもの病死原因の首位はがんだが、まれな疾患であるため、保護者の3割弱しか症状を認識できると感じていない。年間約1900人が診断され、15～24歳でも毎年2300人が新たに診断されている。小児がんは成長に伴い成人よりはるかに急速に進行する。 13歳のアメリア・キャロルさんは昨年、急性骨髄性白血病と診断された。母ジェマさんは、疲労や軽い運動後の息切れを「運動不足や学校後の疲れ」と捉えていたという。しかし、あざが現れたことで異変を悟り、10日以内に診断が確定した。 小児腫瘍科医のボブ・フィリップス教授は、持続し、悪化または拡大する症状の集積に注意するよう促す。極度の疲労、青白い顔色、原因不明のあざ、大きくなり続けるしこりなどだ。専門家は「3のルール」を提唱する。", "vocab": [{"word": "見逃す", "pos": "verb", "zh": "看漏、忽视", "note": "常用于症状、机会等", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "兆候", "pos": "noun", "zh": "征兆、迹象", "note": "比「症状」更早、更模糊", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "集積", "pos": "noun", "zh": "聚集、积累", "note": "症状の集積で“症状扎堆”", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "促す", "pos": "verb", "zh": "促使、敦促", "note": "注意を促す＝提醒注意", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "提唱する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "提倡、倡导", "note": "比「提案」更正式", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "異変", "pos": "noun", "zh": "异常、变故", "note": "異変を悟る＝察觉不对劲", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "しこり", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿块、硬块", "note": "身体にできる硬い塊", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "青白い", "pos": "adj", "zh": "苍白的", "note": "顔色が青白い", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "生存率", "pos": "noun", "zh": "生存率", "note": "医療記事で頻出", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "軽減する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "减轻", "note": "負担を軽減する", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "要する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "需要、花费", "note": "時間を要する", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "腫瘤", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿瘤、肿块", "note": "医学用語", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "寛解", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓解", "note": "症状が一時的に治まること", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "遺産", "pos": "noun", "zh": "遗产、遗留影响", "note": "ここでは「遺志」の意味", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "啓発", "pos": "noun", "zh": "启发、宣传", "note": "啓発活動＝提高意识活动", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "息を引き取る", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "去世、断气", "note": "婉曲的な死の表現", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for", "summary_original": "Although rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK.", "body_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for\nBy\nCamilla Horrox\nHealth reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nAlthough rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK - yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\nAbout 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed with cancer each year, according to Cancer Research UK.\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll, who is now in remission, was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) last year.\nHer mum Gemma recalls the initial symptoms being easy to overlook.\nThere was the tiredness, she says, and being out of breath after mild exercise but both were put down to being unfit and just post-school fatigue.\nIt was only when Amelia started presenting with bruises Gemma knew something wasn't right - within 10 days Amelia got her leukaemia diagnosis.\nWhat are the symptoms to look out for?\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, says it's key for parents to look out for clusters of symptoms that may be persisting, getting worse or bigger.\nConsider contacting a doctor over the following:\nExtreme tiredness\nLooking really pale\nBruises that you can't explain\nLumps and bumps that are getting bigger or not going away and growing over time\nCancer in children develops much more quickly than in adults - effectively due to them growing.\nExperts like Phillips at CCLG, the Children and Young People's Cancer Association, are urging people to follow a national campaign aiming to raise awareness and improve diagnosis times.\nIt includes a rule-of-three for professionals: If a child has had three persistent symptoms, or has visited their doctor three times, or has had three weeks of unexplained symptoms consider making a referral.\nAlthough, it's important to note survival rates have risen significantly since the 1970s, with eight out of 10 children surviving beyond five to 10 years or more after diagnosis.\nFor Amelia, her cancer journey began with a single red bruise that came out of nowhere, says Gemma.\nThree days later, she had bruises down both of her shins on both legs, which were sore and raised.\n\"We'd gone to the opticians for a routine appointment and they noticed that she'd got some burst blood vessels but they weren't sure why,\" she adds.\nWithin 10 days, doctors had confirmed what type of leukaemia Amelia had and she was admitted to hospital to begin treatment.\n\"On the day that they confirmed what she had, I sat her down and she said to me 'have I got cancer?' and I said yes you have. She asked me if she was going to die.\"\n\"I said no, they're doing everything they can, they've got a plan in place and the treatment is going to do everything it can to beat the cancer.\"\nAmelia is currently in remission, undergoing regular bone marrow and blood tests every three months, and has returned to school.\nPhillips says survival rates for childhood cancer in the UK are over 80% but that even quicker diagnosis would lead to less intensive treatments.\n'Raising awareness is his legacy'\nAround the UK, about half the children diagnosed with cancer get their diagnosis within two weeks, he says.\n\"It's pretty quick but we want the other half to not have the wait of 16, 18, 20, 25 weeks from those first symptoms to get a diagnosis.\"\nCancer Research UK data shows that there are more than 400,000 new cases of cancer diagnosed every year in the UK, with only 54% being told within the 28-day NHS target window, according to the British Medical Journal (BMJ).\nAn additional 2,300 teenagers and young adults (aged 15–24) are diagnosed each year.\nLynsey Swinscoe is mum to Jake, who died of soft tissue cancer aged 13. Jake was diagnosed with stage three, fusion positive alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma in July 2023.\nBefore his diagnosis, Jake's initial symptoms of a stuffy nose and watery eyes were put down to hayfever, and falling asleep after school was thought to be just end-of-term tiredness.\n\"It was only when he started to get a swelling across the bridge of his nose that we thought right, we need to take him to the GP,\" Lynsey says.\n\"But at that point we didn't really think the fatigue and hayfever symptoms were related.\"\nA mass was found in Jake's nose and nasal cavity and he was diagnosed with soft tissue sarcoma. He completed nine rounds of chemotherapy and proton beam radiation.\nAfter suffering seizures and feeling very unwell from the chemotherapy, Jake opted out of continuing treatment.\nHe passed away peacefully at home on 26 April 2024, just two weeks before his 14th birthday.\n\"I think the main thing with Jake is he was not scared to die. We had lots of conversations about dying and what it might feel like to die and the only thing that he was sort of scared about was, 'are all the people going to remember me? Is everyone just going to forget who I am?'\n\"That's why I'm doing this, to make sure that people know who Jake is, to make sure people know how incredible he was. And so with that comes the raising awareness, which I think, that's his legacy.\"\nRelated topics\nLeukaemia\nCancer\nChild health\nNHS\nHealth\nChildhood cancer\nMore on this story\nMother writes book to help children with cancer\nPublished\n4 October\nGirl, 7, rings bell to mark end of cancer treatment\nPublished\n1 October\n'This doesn't happen to us' - mum on child's cancer\nPublished\n12 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:45:05.386127", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "英国", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.6, "sm": 125, "em": 600, "cs": 0, "ce": 2, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "で", "s": 0.613, "e": 0.713, "sm": 612, "em": 712, "cs": 2, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "は", "s": 0.725, "e": 0.875, "sm": 725, "em": 875, "cs": 3, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "15", "s": 0.887, "e": 1.312, "sm": 887, "em": 1312, "cs": 4, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "歳", "s": 1.312, "e": 1.525, "sm": 1312, "em": 1525, "cs": 6, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "未満", "s": 1.538, "e": 1.85, "sm": 1537, "em": 1850, "cs": 7, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "の", "s": 1.863, "e": 2.0, "sm": 1862, "em": 2000, "cs": 9, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "子ども", "s": 2.013, "e": 2.388, "sm": 2012, "em": 2387, "cs": 10, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "の", "s": 2.4, "e": 2.512, "sm": 2400, "em": 2512, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "病死", "s": 2.525, "e": 2.913, "sm": 2525, "em": 2912, "cs": 14, "ce": 16, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "原因", "s": 2.925, "e": 3.31